{
  "configuration_id": "config_015",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_005 (multimodal, singleprofile, pca_n2000, imagenet_vit_l, weighted) achieves 0.5318 acc, only modestly above RNA-alone (config_050, 0.4849), while image-alone (config_022) is very weak (0.2266). This indicates RNA is the dominant signal and the image modality contributes little with the current setup. The key uncertainty is whether the RNA representation itself can be improved: we have only tested a linear PCA encoder (pca_n2000) and do not know if a learned deep RNA encoder (scgpt) would capture richer cell-type structure. Question: config_015 changes only the RNA encoder from pca_n2000 to scgpt while keeping everything else identical to config_005 (singleprofile, imagenet_vit_l, no aggregation, weighted fusion), giving a clean single-variable comparison. Why this is the best next experiment: RNA drives most of the predictive signal, so improving its representation has the highest expected leverage on overall accuracy. If scgpt outperforms PCA, it validates learned RNA encoders and directs further exploration (e.g., scgpt + multiprofile); if it does not, we can rule out that axis and redirect budget toward fusion strategy or multiprofile aggregation. This isolates one critical design choice without confounding variables."
}

